{"name":"Blue Atoll","hex":"#00b3e1","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"atoll","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of island consisting of a ribbon reef that nearly or entirely surrounds a lagoon and supports, in most cases, one to many islets on the reef platform."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/atoll"}],"entries":[{"title":"Atoll","blocks":[{"text":"An atoll is a ring-shaped island, including a coral rim that encircles a lagoon. There may be coral islands or cays on the rim. Atolls are located in warm tropical or subtropical parts of the oceans and seas where corals can develop. Most of the approximately 440 atolls in the world are in the Pacific Ocean."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Tetiaroa_from_sky.JPG/330px-Tetiaroa_from_sky.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atoll","wikidata":"Q42523"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/On_the_structure_and_distribution_of_coral_reefs_BHL40453231.jpg/500px-On_the_structure_and_distribution_of_coral_reefs_BHL40453231.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/On_the_structure_and_distribution_of_coral_reefs_BHL40453231.jpg/1280px-On_the_structure_and_distribution_of_coral_reefs_BHL40453231.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/On_the_structure_and_distribution_of_coral_reefs_BHL40453231.jpg/500px-On_the_structure_and_distribution_of_coral_reefs_BHL40453231.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Map from Charles Darwin's 1842 The Structure and Distribution of Coral Reefs showing the world's major groups of atolls and coral reefs","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOn_the_structure_and_distribution_of_coral_reefs_BHL40453231.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Penrhyn_Aerial_EFS_1280.jpg/250px-Penrhyn_Aerial_EFS_1280.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Penrhyn_Aerial_EFS_1280.jpg/500px-Penrhyn_Aerial_EFS_1280.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Penrhyn_Aerial_EFS_1280.jpg/250px-Penrhyn_Aerial_EFS_1280.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser